<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kasubi Tombs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1882</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kasubi Tombs on Kasubi Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> are the burial place of four kabakas of Buganda: Mutesa I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daudi-chwa-ii">Daudi Chwa II</a> and Mutesa II. The main building, a large domed structure of wood, reed and thatch, was built as Mutesa I's palace in 1882 and became his tomb in 1884. It was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2001, and badly damaged by fire in 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasubi-tombs.jpg" alt="Kasubi Tombs" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: not not phil from SF, CA, US · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kampala_Kasubi_Tombs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
